---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevFormatXmlJson from '@/components/tools/dev/DevFormatXmlJson.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="json-to-xml">
  <DevFormatXmlJson client:load direction="json-to-xml" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How JSON structures become XML</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>JSON</th><th>XML</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>&#123;"a": "x"&#125;</code></td><td><code>&lt;a&gt;x&lt;/a&gt;</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>&#123;"@id": "1"&#125;</code> on an object</td><td><code>id="1"</code> as an attribute of that element</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>&#123;"#text": "x"&#125;</code> alongside other keys</td><td>The element&rsquo;s text content</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>&#123;"a": [1, 2]&#125;</code></td><td><code>&lt;a&gt;1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a&gt;2&lt;/a&gt;</code>: the element repeats</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>&#123;"a": null&#125;</code></td><td><code>&lt;a/&gt;</code>: an empty element</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>true</code>, <code>42</code>, <code>1.5</code></td><td>Their text form. XML has no types</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A bare top-level array</td><td>Wrapped in the root element, one <code>&lt;item&gt;</code> per entry</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>The single-root rule</h2>
    <p>
      Each XML document must have exactly one root element. JSON has no such rule, so a JSON to XML
      conversion must add one when the input is an array or an object with several top-level keys. When
      your JSON has exactly one top-level key (the shape that the <a href="/xml-to-json">XML to JSON</a>
      converter gives), that key becomes the root and no extra wrapper is added. Otherwise the tool uses
      the root name field.
    </p>

    <h2>Why some keys get renamed</h2>
    <p>
      XML element names have many more rules than JSON keys. A name must start with a letter, underscore
      or colon. It can contain only letters, digits, hyphens, underscores, colons and full stops. It must
      not begin with the letters &ldquo;xml&rdquo; in any mix of cases, which is reserved. JSON keys have
      no restrictions: spaces, emoji, empty strings and leading digits are all legal.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tool replaces illegal characters with underscores and adds a prefix before a leading digit. The
      list under the output shows each rename, because a rename without notice can make a downstream
      XPath stop matching.
    </p>

    <h2>Escaping is handled for you</h2>
    <p>
      The tool escapes <code>&amp;</code>, <code>&lt;</code> and <code>&gt;</code> in values when it
      writes them into element text, and also escapes double quotes in attribute values. That is the
      correct place to escape: at insertion, on the value alone. Do not escape your JSON first, or you
      get <code>&amp;amp;amp;</code> in the output.
    </p>

    <h2>When JSON to XML is the right move</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Legacy integrations: a modern service gives JSON, and a SOAP endpoint, an EDI gateway or a mainframe interface wants XML.</li>
      <li>Feeds: RSS, Atom and sitemaps are XML formats with fixed element names. Generate the structure here and adjust the names.</li>
      <li>Config formats: Maven, Android, .NET and Spring all read XML, so a generator that works in JSON must write it.</li>
      <li>XSLT: to transform data with XSLT, it must be XML first.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What the output is not</h2>
    <p>
      It is well-formed XML, not schema-valid XML. Element names, order and cardinality come from your
      JSON, not from an XSD, so a service that expects a specific grammar will probably reject the first
      attempt. Use this to get the shape and the escaping correct, then change the names to match the
      target schema, and validate with the <a href="/xml-validator">XML validator</a> before you send.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
